      "[Expert R1] (Detect atomicity violations in the code.)\n```json\n{\n    \"violations\": [\n        {\n            \"sharedVariable\": \"g\",\n            \"accessPattern\": [\"Read\", \"Write\", \"Read\"],\n            \"lines\": [13, 8, 14]\n        },\n        {\n            \"sharedVariable\": \"g\",\n            \"accessPattern\": [\"Read\", \"Write\", \"Read\"],\n            \"lines\": [13, 8, 16]\n        }\n    ]\n}\n```\n\n[System] Expert first round completed in 4.43 seconds\n\n[Judge R1] (Validate the expert's defect report and check control/interrupt conditions.)\nLet me analyze each read/write operation for the global variable `g`.\n\n## Step 1: Check Control Conditions\n\n### Read operation at line 13: `if (g)`\n- This is a read operation in the main function\n- The control condition is simply the evaluation of `g` in the if statement\n- `g` is initialized to 0 at line 5\n- This read operation will always be executed when `svp_simple_634_001_main()` runs\n\n### Write operation at line 8: `g = 1`\n- This is a write operation in ISR `svp_simple_634_001_isr_1`\n- There are no control conditions guarding this operation - it's executed unconditionally when the ISR runs\n\n### Read operation at line 14: `__VERIFIER_assert(g)`\n- This is a read operation in the main function\n- It's guarded by the condition `if (g)` from line 13\n- This operation is executed only when the condition `g != 0` is true\n\n### Read operation at line 16: `__VERIFIER_assert(!g)`\n- This is a read operation in the main function\n- It's guarded by the else branch of the condition `if (g)` from line 13\n- This operation is executed only when the condition `g == 0` is true\n\n## Step 2: Pay Attention to Control Condition Changes\n\nThe ISR `svp_simple_634_001_isr_1` can change the value of `g` from 0 to 1. This could potentially affect the control flow in the main function.\n\n## Step 3: Check Interrupt Status\n\nThere are no `disable_isr()` or `enable_isr()` calls in the code. This means:\n- All interrupts are enabled by default\n- The ISR `svp_simple_634_001_isr_1` can preempt the main function at any point\n\n## Step 4: Generate the Final Report\n\n### Analysis Summary:\n1. **Line 8 (Write)**: The write operation `g = 1` in the ISR can always execute when the ISR is triggered\n2. **Line 13 (Read)**: The read operation `if (g)` in main will always execute\n3. **Line 14 (Read)**: The read operation `__VERIFIER_assert(g)` can execute if `g != 0`\n4. **Line 16 (Read)**: The read operation `__VERIFIER_assert(!g)` can execute if `g == 0`\n\nSince interrupts are always enabled, the ISR can preempt the main function at any point, potentially changing `g` from 0 to 1 between the read at line 13 and the subsequent reads at lines 14 or 16.\n\nThe reported atomicity violations are valid because:\n- The ISR can change `g` between the read at line 13 and the reads at lines 14 or 16\n- This creates a race condition where the assertion might fail\n\n```json\n{\n    \"violations\": [\n        {\n            \"sharedVariable\": \"g\",\n            \"accessPattern\": [\"Read\", \"Write\", \"Read\"],\n            \"lines\": [13, 8, 14]\n        },\n        {\n            \"sharedVariable\": \"g\",\n            \"accessPattern\": [\"Read\", \"Write\", \"Read\"],\n            \"lines\": [13, 8, 16]\n        }\n    ]\n}\n```\n\n[Expert R2] (Refine the report based on judge feedback, re-check for missed or false defects.)\n```json\n{\n    \"violations\": [\n        {\n            \"sharedVariable\": \"g\",\n            \"accessPattern\": [\"Read\", \"Write\", \"Read\"],\n            \"lines\": [13, 8, 14]\n        },\n        {\n            \"sharedVariable\": \"g\",\n            \"accessPattern\": [\"Read\", \"Write\", \"Read\"],\n            \"lines\": [13, 8, 16]\n        }\n    ]\n}\n```\n\n"
